== git clean: does -n win over -f, in every order?
$ git clean -n
Would remove probe-file.txt
-> exit 0
   probe files: still there
$ git clean -nd
Would remove probe-dir/
Would remove probe-file.txt
-> exit 0
   probe files: still there
$ git clean -nf
Would remove probe-file.txt
-> exit 0
   probe files: still there
$ git clean -fn
Would remove probe-file.txt
-> exit 0
   probe files: still there
$ git clean -n -f -d
Would remove probe-dir/
Would remove probe-file.txt
-> exit 0
   probe files: still there
$ git clean --dry-run -f -d
Would remove probe-dir/
Would remove probe-file.txt
-> exit 0
   probe files: still there
$ git clean -f -d --dry-run
Would remove probe-dir/
Would remove probe-file.txt
-> exit 0
   probe files: still there
$ git clean -ndx
Would remove probe-dir/
Would remove probe-file.txt
-> exit 0
   probe files: still there

